d:[["$","title","0",{"children":"Processes, Threads, and Why Scheduling Matters - Dusti Johnson"}],["$","meta","1",{"name":"description","content":"Processes, PCBs, threads, scheduling, mutexes, and semaphores explain how CPUs share work and why concurrency is powerful but difficult."}],["$","link","2",{"rel":"icon","href":"/favicon.ico?60f8fdf2017983bc","type":"image/x-icon","sizes":"16x16"}],["$","$Lf","3",{}]]
10:I[7852,["250","static/chunks/250-b3955559750b3597.js","938","static/chunks/938-8839081fe57c7110.js","852","static/chunks/852-20266caf84033988.js","60","static/chunks/app/blog/future-of-web-development/page-fa0eef90e6f208df.js"],"RootLayout"]
6:["$","$L10",null,{"children":[["$","article",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 lg:px-8 mt-24 sm:mt-32 lg:mt-40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-2xl lg:max-w-none","children":[["$","$L4",null,{"children":["$","header",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-5xl flex-col text-center","children":[["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-6 font-display text-5xl font-medium tracking-tight text-balance text-neutral-950 sm:text-6xl","children":"Processes, Threads, and Why Scheduling Matters"}],["$","time",null,{"dateTime":"2026-03-14","className":"order-first text-sm text-neutral-950","children":"March 14, 2026"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-neutral-950","children":["by ","Dusti Johnson",", ","Developer"]}]]}]}],["$","$L4",null,{"children":["$","div",null,{"className":"*:mx-auto *:max-w-3xl *:first:mt-0! *:last:mb-0! mt-24 sm:mt-32 lg:mt-40","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"typography","children":["\n","\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"The CPU only runs what is loaded into context"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"A program sitting on disk is not doing anything. It becomes a process when the\noperating system gives it memory, resources, and a place in the scheduling\nsystem. To actually run, the CPU needs the process's execution context: the\nprogram counter, register values, stack pointer, memory mappings, and current\nstate."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"The important detail is that a CPU core does not magically run every process at\nonce. At any instant, a core is executing one stream of instructions. What makes\na computer feel like it is running many programs at once is fast switching,\nmultiple cores, and the operating system's ability to pause and resume work."}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"The PCB is what makes stopping safe"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"The process control block is the data structure that lets the OS stop a process\nwithout losing it. When a process is interrupted, blocked, or preempted, the OS\nhas to save enough information to continue later as if nothing happened."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"That saved information lives in the PCB: process state, program counter, CPU\nregisters, scheduling priority, memory information, accounting data, and\nresource handles. When the scheduler chooses that process again, the OS restores\nthe saved context and the CPU continues from the stored program counter."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"This is also why multi-core CPUs can run multiple processes truly in parallel.\nEach core has its own current execution context, and the OS keeps track of which\nprocess or thread is assigned where. The PCB gives the scheduler a stable record\nof each process even while different cores are running different work."}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Scheduling is not a promise"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"It is tempting to imagine processes taking turns in a neat order, but real\nscheduling is not guaranteed that way. A process usually cannot assume it will\nrun next, run soon, or run for a specific amount of time."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"The scheduler may interrupt a process because its time slice expired, a higher\npriority process became ready, an I/O operation completed, or the system needs\nto rebalance work across cores. Scheduling policies try to balance goals like\nresponsiveness, throughput, fairness, priority, and CPU utilization, but the\nexact order is still something application code should not depend on."}],"\n"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"my-10 pl-8 relative before:absolute after:absolute before:bg-neutral-950 after:bg-neutral-950/10 before:top-0 before:left-0 before:h-6 before:w-px after:top-8 after:bottom-0 after:left-0 after:w-px","children":["$L11","$L12"]}],"$L13"]}]}]]}]}],"$L14","$L15"]}]
